2. CULTURAL HERITAGE, HISTORY AND ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS

2.1 No ownership claim over cultures

Kitty Crow and Online Arcana claim no ownership, authorship or exclusive right
over any people, culture, language, religion, history, folklore, mythology,
oral tradition, community knowledge, artistic form, musical tradition,
traditional name, garment, instrument, symbol, ritual, belief, archaeological
heritage or public-domain work from which the project draws inspiration.

Copyright claims are limited to protectable original code and original
expression created for Online Arcana. Nothing in this licence limits any
community's right to practise, preserve, teach, develop, reclaim or represent
its own heritage, nor anyone's lawful use of historical facts, public-domain
material or independently created cultural expression.

2.2 Attested inspirations and gratitude

The author attests that the following histories, peoples and living traditions
consciously informed the fictional reader designs and respectfully thanks them
for their contribution to the beautiful and diverse world in which this work
was created:

- Celtic histories of continental Europe, especially the ancient Helvetii, in
  the inspiration for Brennos;
- Yoruba histories and living cultures in the inspiration for Yejide;
- Māori history, language and living culture in the inspiration for Ngaru;
- Yayoi-period Japanese history and archaeology, together with wider Japanese
  artistic traditions, in the inspiration for Ame;
- Inca history and living Quechua, Aymara and wider Andean cultures in the
  inspiration for Amaru;
- pre-Islamic Persian histories and the wider continuum of Persian cultural
  traditions in the inspiration for Nahid;
- Mexica history and living Nahua cultures in the inspiration for Mictli; and
- Andalusian flamenco and cante jondo traditions, including the profound
  contributions of Gitano and Romani communities, in the inspiration for
  Selena.

This acknowledgement is gratitude, not a claim that the characters reproduce,
represent or speak for those cultures.

2.3 Conquest, oppression, survival and continuity

Online Arcana does not describe living peoples as vanished, extinct or
eradicated.

The Inca and Mexica states were destroyed through Spanish conquest, while
their peoples and descendants endured colonial violence, dispossession, forced
labour, forced conversion and cultural suppression. Quechua, Aymara, Nahua and
other Indigenous communities survived and continue to preserve, renew and
develop their cultures.

Māori endured colonisation, warfare, land confiscation and alienation, and the
suppression of te reo Māori and tikanga. Māori remain the living Indigenous
people of Aotearoa New Zealand, and their struggles for land, language,
sovereignty, justice and cultural flourishing continue.

Yoruba peoples and cultures endured enslavement through the Atlantic slave
trade and later colonial rule, while sustaining living traditions across
Africa and the diaspora.

Gitano and Romani communities have likewise endured centuries of persecution
and marginalisation while making indispensable contributions to flamenco and
many other cultural forms.

These acknowledgements are not exhaustive. They are included to recognise that
cultural beauty cannot be separated from the people who carried it through
violence, exclusion and attempts at suppression.

2.4 Fiction, responsibility and no endorsement

The readers are fictional creations and are not historical reconstructions,
religious authorities, cultural representatives or substitutes for learning
from members and knowledge keepers of the cultures acknowledged above. No
affiliation, approval or endorsement by any people, nation, iwi, community,
institution or cultural authority is claimed or implied.

Any error, simplification, stereotype or insensitive choice in the project is
the responsibility of the author, not of the cultures that inspired it.

